          "settings": {
            "number": "1",
            "title": "Why breastfed babies struggle",
            "body": "<p><strong>A mother's breast isn't fixed.</strong> It softens, compresses, changes shape, and meets your baby's mouth as they feed.</p><p>A bottle nipple doesn't do any of that.</p><p>It doesn't soften around your baby's mouth. It doesn't adjust when they pull, pause, or change pressure. It just sits there: same shape, same angle, same flow.</p><p>So when the nipple is too long, too narrow, or sitting awkwardly in the mouth, your baby has to work around the bottle.</p><p>That's when a normal feed can start looking wrong fast. <strong>The latch slips. Milk runs down the side.</strong> And a hungry baby suddenly looks like they're fighting the bottle.</p>"
          }
        },
        "image-2": {
